@@ -0,0 +1,87 @@
+# Radxa Zero 3W Pollen Robot HAT audio candidate
+
+For the Radxa Zero 3W with the Pollen Robot HAT, codec at 0x18 and a 12 MHz
+codec oscillator. This is not for the bare board, the beta board (RK809 and
+face mic), or an unconfirmed custom audio circuit.
+
+## Build and update boundary
+
+Use a working upstream kas/Docker build environment and the daemon release
+as described in README. Opt into the separate HAT image:
+
+```sh
+KAS_FILES=kas/microduck.yml:kas/zero3-hat-audio.yml \
+  DAEMON_VERSION=0.20.1 scripts/build-local.sh build --target microduck-image
+```
+
+Check that the build's kernel configuration includes all five settings in
+`microduck-zero3-hat-audio.cfg`; the recipe already fails if any is lost.
+Check the booted slot's `/boot/microduck.dtb` (not only a loose DTBO) and the
+image package manifest for `microduck-zero3-hat-audio` and
+`alsa-utils-amixer`. This changes the DTB, which is also used to derive the
+boot partition. Do not transplant the change into a stock updater bundle,
+or treat a daemon-only update as a driver update. Build a separate WIC and
+verify both slots, boot, and rollback on a spare card before distribution.
+The recipe does not issue a flash command.
+
+## Wiring and behavior
+
+* RK3566 I2S3 stays on its stock M0 PCM pins; this enables its existing DAI.
+  The RK3566 is the bit-clock/frame master, its sysclk is 12.288 MHz, and
+  the codec clock reference is the HAT's separate 12 MHz oscillator.
+* Header 3/5 are GPIO1_A0/A1; the new `i2c-gpio` bus uses open drain and
+  the HAT pullups. There is no assumption about its Linux bus number. The
+  built-in codec driver binds to `ti,tlv320aic3x`; that compatible preserves
+  the upstream HAT mixer control names. USB-C PD's FUSB302 stays on the
+  existing hardware I2C3 M1 (GPIO3_B5/B6). This only preserves topology;
+  actual USB-C behavior still needs a board test.
+* The package initializes only card `aic3104`, output and MIC3R routing,
+  with a conservative 0 dB line stage. It logs a missing card and exits
+  after a short wait. It is ordered before robotd, not a hard dependency.
+* No ToF driver, address, bus route, app audio permission, recording
+  automation, sound bank, or motion configuration is changed. The existing
+  voice bank service and stock `audio.pet_detect` policy remain in force.
+
+## Bench verification (no motor commands)
+
+1. Keep the robot stationary with servo power off. Check the card and
+   its playback and capture directions before changing application settings:
+
+   ```sh
+   cat /proc/asound/cards
+   aplay -l
+   arecord -l
+   journalctl -b -u microduck-zero3-hat-audio -u microduck-voice-bank -u robotd --no-pager
+   amixer -c aic3104 scontents
+   ```
+
+2. Use a known WAV to verify speaker routing with the duck at a distance.
+   Then test the daemon separately with `robotctl quack`.
+
+   ```sh
+   aplay -q -D plughw:CARD=aic3104,DEV=0 /opt/robot/daemon/current/emotions/yes.wav
+   ```
+
+3. Tell the nearby participants before enabling the mic. While no other
+   process holds capture, record locally for five seconds at stereo 48 kHz
+   so the HAT's right channel can be checked. Use a private temp directory
+   and remove this particular debug recording when done.
+
+   ```sh
+   scratch=$(mktemp -d)
+   arecord -q -D hw:CARD=aic3104,DEV=0 -f S16_LE -r 48000 -c 2 -t wav -d 5 "$scratch/mic.wav"
+   aplay -q -D plughw:CARD=aic3104,DEV=0 "$scratch/mic.wav"
+   rm -f "$scratch/mic.wav"
+   rmdir "$scratch"
+   ```
+
+   If capture answers with `Device or resource busy`, find the current
+   consumer instead of restarting robotd on a running robot. If the left
+   channel is quiet, inspect the right channel before changing gain: MIC3R
+   is intended to be right-only. A headphone/speaker test alone does not
+   show what the daemon's 16 kHz mono software conversion receives.
+
+4. Reboot once, confirm the mixer route is restored, test the stock USB-C
+   power/role behavior, and confirm BLE, camera, motor bus and any existing
+   ToF wiring have not regressed. The image is not hardware-accepted until
+   microphone levels and the actual speaker are both checked on this HAT.
